\begin_layout Standard
Bioconductor has served as the gold standard for high-throughput genomic analysis by providing standardized data structures and reproducible statistical methods,
 where as Python has quickly become the primary environment for deep learning,
 image analysis,
 and general-purpose data science.
 This presents a challenge to the scientific community,
 navigating complex,
 fragile,
 and often inefficient interoperability solutions to leverage the strengths of both worlds.
 We present BiocPy,
 a software framework and ecosystem designed to bridge this gap by reengineering the foundational Bioconductor data structures as native Python data representations.
 BiocPy provides Python-native implementations that adheres to Bioconductor’s S4 class semantics,
 including ingestion of serialized R data objects.
 This approach allows developers to build robust,
 polyglot workflows that leverage the strengths of both ecosystems without compromising data integrity.
 All packages in the BiocPy ecosystem are published to Python's Package Index (PyPI) and code available on GitHub 
\begin_inset CommandInset href
LatexCommand href
target "http://github.com/BiocPy"

\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section
Introduction
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Bioinformatics is an interdisciplinary field at the intersection of molecular biology,
 statistics,
 and computer science.
 Bioconductor
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "gentleman_bioconductor_2004"
literal "false"

\end_inset

 built upon the R programming language,
 was designed with a primary focus on statistical analysis and visualization of genomic data.
 Over the last two decades,
 it has grown into a repository of over 2,000 packages,
 conforming to a strict set of shared data structures and design principles.
 Objects such as 
\begin_inset Flex Code
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout
GenomicRanges
\end_layout

\end_inset


\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "lawrence_software_2013"
literal "false"

\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Flex Code
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout
SummarizedExperiment
\end_layout

\end_inset


\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "noauthor_summarizedexperiment_nodate"
literal "false"

\end_inset

 provide a common currency for data exchange,
 allowing developers to quickly build and iterate methods without reinventing new data structures.
 For researchers focused on rigorous statistical testing methods and established genomic workflows,
 Bioconductor remains the standard environment to perform these analyses.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Python has emerged over the last decade as the dominant language of data science and machine learning (ML).
 Powered by libraries such as NumPy
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "harris_array_2020"
literal "false"

\end_inset

,
 PyTorch
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "paszke_pytorch_2019"
literal "false"

\end_inset

,
 and TensorFlow
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "abadi_tensorflow_2016"
literal "false"

\end_inset

,
 it provides flexibility,
 and integration with modern computing paradigms (distributed computing,
 CUDA programming).
 In the context of genomics,
 Python has seen a surge in popularity driven by the rise of single-cell analysis through the scverse efforts
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "virshup_scverse_2023"
literal "false"

\end_inset

,
 where large-scale analyses benefit significantly from ML libraries e.g.
 rapids-singlecell
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "dicks_scverserapids_singlecell_2024"
literal "false"

\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The coexistence of these two powerful computational ecosystems creates a fragmentation problem.
 A typical modern genomic workflow might require the robust methods available in Bioconductor (R),
 followed by the application of a deep learning models or inference methods available in Python.
 Researchers traditionally bridge this divide through file-based exchange (CSV),
 which loses metadata and incurs serialization costs.
 Reimplementing statistical methods in Python often leads to subtle algorithmic divergence and incompatibility with standard data models.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
BiocPy addresses this gap,
 rather than forcing R to run inside Python (or vice-versa),
 or creating an entirely new,
 incompatible Python-native standard,
 BiocPy ports Bioconductor’s core class definitions into Python.
 A 
\begin_inset Flex Code
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout
SummarizedExperiment
\end_layout

\end_inset

 object in BiocPy is designed to have the same slots,
 and the same behavioral guarantees as its R counterpart.
 This ensures that the conceptual data model remains consistent,
 reducing the cognitive burden for researchers and developers transitioning between languages.
 By adhering to Bioconductor’s design principles,
 such as the separation of assay data from row/column metadata and the use of functional programming patterns,
 BiocPy enables a workflow where data can round-trip between R and Python.
 These efforts create a bridge between computational platforms and enables researches to focus their time on biological questions rather than technical challenges.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The BiocPy framework is targeted at developers who require the reliability of Bioconductor's data structures within a Python environment.
 BiocPy is not the only attempt to solve the R/Python divide,
 but its approach is unique.
 Unlike rpy2
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "noauthor_rpy2rpy2_2025"
literal "false"

\end_inset

,
 which is powerful but heavy due to embedding an R instance,
 it's often difficult to configure in containerized environments (Docker/Singularity).
 BiocPy is lightweight and native.
 While 
\begin_inset Flex Code
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout
AnnData
\end_layout

\end_inset


\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "virshup_anndata_2024"
literal "false"

\end_inset

 is well-suited for single-cell data,
 it lacks the generality of 
\begin_inset Flex Code
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout
SummarizedExperiment
\end_layout

\end_inset

 (which handles any matrix data) and does not support 
\begin_inset Flex Code
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout
GenomicRanges
\end_layout

\end_inset

 (or range based operations).
 BiocPy supports a broader spectrum of genomic data types.
 Finally,
 while writing genomic tools in pure Python is possible,
 it often leads to poor performance or lack of standardization.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section
Core Data Structures
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The development of BiocPy is not just a translation of R code into Python,
 it is an adoption of Bioconductor’s design philosophy into a Pythonic context.
 The development of the ecosystem is guided by adherence to specific design principles that prioritize reliability,
 interoperability and usability.
 The goal is not to compete with existing Python data structures and scientific stack,
 but to provide objects that are structurally compatible with Bioconductor.
 While BiocPy objects can be converted to popular Python packages (e.g.,
 pandas 
\begin_inset Flex Code
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout
DataFrames
\end_layout

\end_inset

,
 
\begin_inset Flex Code
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout
AnnData
\end_layout

\end_inset

) for integration with standard tools,
 these conversions are often lossy.
 BiocPy structures are required to maintain full fidelity with the original R objects to support cross language workflows.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Adopting Bioconductor's design philosophy,
 BiocPy enforces a functional programming paradigm where operations return new instances rather than modifying objects in-place.
 This immutability safeguards against side effects common in complex genomic pipelines,
 offering a robust alternative to the mutable patterns often found in Python.
 Although conceptually rooted in R,
 BiocPy implementations take advantage of existing Python scientific stack (NumPy,
 SciPy).
 This design maintains a lean dependency footprint,
 facilitating easy installation and minimizing the environment conflicts that frequently plague bioinformatics software.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection
BiocFrame:
 A Flexible DataFrame
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The 
\begin_inset Flex Code
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout
BiocFrame
\end_layout

\end_inset

 class mimics the functionality of Bioconductor's 
\begin_inset Flex Code
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout
DFrame
\end_layout

\end_inset

.
 its primary role is to serve as a robust container for genomic metadata within the BiocPy ecosystem.
 The pandas 
\begin_inset Flex Code
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout
DataFrame
\end_layout

\end_inset

 is ubiquitous in most analysis workflows.
 However,
 for genomic data,
 pandas presents several limitations that BiocFrame addresses.
 Unlike a standard pandas 
\begin_inset Flex Code
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout
DataFrame
\end_layout

\end_inset

,
 which often forces aggressive type coercion (e.g.,
 casting integers to floats in the presence of NaNs),
 BiocFrame preserves original data types.
 BiocFrame is designed to support arbitrarily nested columns natively,
 such as a columns that are themselves a 
\begin_inset Flex Code
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout
BiocFrame
\end_layout

\end_inset

 objects or a list of 
\begin_inset Flex Code
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout
GenomicRanges
\end_layout

\end_inset

 objects.
 This allows for the construction of recursive,
 hierarchical models that accurately reflect the biological data,
 rather than flattening everything into a 2D table.
 BiocFrame includes robust utilities for conversion to and from pandas or polar objects,
 because researchers often need to leverage these for general data cleaning or visualization tasks before moving into Bioconductor-specific workflows.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection
GenomicRanges
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Interval arithmetic is central to genomic analysis.
 The most crucial feature of Bioconductor is its handling of genomic intervals via the GenomicRanges
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "lawrence_software_2013"
literal "false"

\end_inset

 package.
 In Python,
 interval arithmetic has historically been handled by wrapping command-line tools like bedtools
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "quinlan_bedtools_2010"
literal "false"

\end_inset

 or using standalone libraries like pyranges
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "stovner_pyranges_2020"
literal "false"

\end_inset

.
 BiocPy brings a native,
 in-memory implementation of GenomicRanges to Python.
 Unlike a generic 
\begin_inset Flex Code
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout
DataFrame
\end_layout

\end_inset

,
 using a specialized 
\begin_inset Flex Code
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout
GenomicRanges
\end_layout

\end_inset

 object provides developers with structural guarantees regarding coordinate systems,
 strand information,
 and sequence lengths as shown in Figure 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "fig:GR"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"
nolink "false"

\end_inset

.
 This structure allows for the validation of operations,
 such as ensuring ranges do not extend beyond chromosome boundaries.
 BiocPy’s 
\begin_inset Flex Code
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout
GenomicRanges
\end_layout

\end_inset

 abstracts the coordinate system,
 providing methods to import from commonly used annotation file formats or R objects while maintaining internal consistency.
 The availability of methods like 
\begin_inset Flex Code
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout
findOverlaps
\end_layout

\end_inset

,
 
\begin_inset Flex Code
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout
nearest
\end_layout

\end_inset

,
 and 
\begin_inset Flex Code
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout
resize
\end_layout

\end_inset

 in native Python allows for complex analyses.
 For example,
 one can load a set of ChIP-seq peaks and a set of gene annotations,
 and in a few lines of Python,
 determine which genes have peaks in their promoter regions,
 handling strand-specificity automatically.
 GenomicRanges serves as the foundation for performing interval operations in native Python,
 mirroring the functionality available in R.
\end_layout

