Containers for Experimental Data
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
To encapsulate the complexity of high-throughput experiments,
 BiocPy provides container classes that bind assay data to metadata.
 These classes strictly adhere to the Bioconductor S4 model,
 support genomic range operations and facilitate interoperability with R tools as shown in 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "fig:MAE"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"
nolink "false"

\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsubsection

\series bold
SummarizedExperiment (SE) & RangedSummarizedExperiments (RSE)
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The fundamental container for matrix-like data.
 It enforces a geometry where rows represent features (genes) and columns represent samples,
 contrasting with the "observations as rows" convention in scverse/
\begin_inset Flex Code
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout
AnnData
\end_layout

\end_inset

.
 The 
\begin_inset Flex Code
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout
RangeSummarizedExperiment
\end_layout

\end_inset

 class extends SE with an additional 
\begin_inset Flex Code
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout
rowRanges
\end_layout

\end_inset

 (a 
\begin_inset Flex Code
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout
GenomicRanges
\end_layout

\end_inset

 object) slot containing the genomic coordinates for each feature.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsubsection

\series bold
SingleCellExperiment (SCE)
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Extending RSE,
 the 
\begin_inset Flex Code
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout
SingleCellExperiment
\end_layout

\end_inset

 class is tailored for single-cell RNA-seq data.
 It adds slots for "reduced dimensions" (PCA,
 UMAP,
 t-SNE) and "Alternative Experiments" (AltExps).
 In modern single-cell workflows,
 a cell might have RNA data,
 but also spike-in controls,
 or Antibody Derived Tags (CITE-seq).
 AltExps allows these auxiliary matrices to be stored within the main object,
 linked to the same cells but with different feature sets.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsubsection

\series bold
MultiAssayExperiment (MAE)
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
For complex study designs involving multiple assays performed on the same subjects (e.g.,
 a cohort with RNA-seq,
 mutation calls,
 and copy number variants),
 BiocPy implements MultiAssayExperiment
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "noauthor_software_nodate"
literal "false"

\end_inset

.
 This container manages a collection of experiments (which can be 
\begin_inset Flex Code
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout
SummarizedExperiment
\end_layout

\end_inset

 objects or its derivatives) and a sample map that links biological specimens to the specific assays performed on them.
 This structure is vital for multi-omics integration,
 allowing for coordinated subsetting across diverse data modalities.
\begin_inset Newline newline
\end_inset


\begin_inset Newline newline
\end_inset

While converters to 
\begin_inset Flex Code
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout
AnnData
\end_layout

\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Flex Code
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout
MuData
\end_layout

\end_inset

 are lossy and provided for these classes,
 the BiocPy containers are essential for workflows requiring strict adherence to the Bioconductor S4 models,
 particularly when handling complex study designs or genomic range operations.
 These genomic data containers enable both researchers and developers to easily interoperate without having to learn a new system and take advantage of existing scientific ecosystems in Python.
\end_layout

