\begin_layout Subsection
Delayed Arrays
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
BiocPy enables the handling of large-scale datasets through lazy evaluation,
 mirroring Bioconductor’s DelayedArray stack.
 The genomic data containers from Section 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "subsec:Containers-for-Experimental"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"
nolink "false"

\end_inset

 are compatible with 
\begin_inset Flex Code
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout
DelayedArray
\end_layout

\end_inset

 objects in the "assays" slot.
 This allows for operating on on-disk data (e.g.,
 HDF5,
 TileDB) without loading the full dataset into memory.
 While functionally similar to Dask,
 BiocPy’s implementation ensures semantic consistency with R’s DelayedArray operations,
 facilitating interoperability.
 Converters are available to transform these objects into Dask arrays for integration with the broader Python scientific stack.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection
Data Interoperability
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsubsection
Read Serialized RDS files
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
A major barrier in cross-language workflows is the incompatibility of data serialization formats (R’s RDS vs.
 Python’s Pickle).
 BiocPy provides rds2py,
 a package that can read R’s binary serialization format (RDS).
 This allows Python environments to ingest complex S4 objects directly from disk without requiring an installed R runtime (Figure 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "fig:CS_rds2py"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"
nolink "false"

\end_inset

).
 rds2py implements memory-efficient loading strategies rather than performing deep copies where ever possible.
 This is essential when loading large Bioconductor objects for downstream analyses in Python.
 With the availability of Bioconductor classes in Python,
 we can provide access to the same data from R.
\end_layout

