\begin_layout Section
Discussion
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
While BiocPy provides a bridge between ecosystems,
 we acknowledge current limitations relative to the mature Bioconductor project.
 First,
 BiocPy focuses on foundational data structures and core single-cell analysis workflows;
 it does not yet mirror (nor is our intent) the vast repository of over 2,000 specialized packages available in R.
 Second,
 the framework prioritizes strict type stability and object-oriented consistency (mirroring S4 semantics).
 This design philosophy,
 while essential for reproducibility,
 may present a learning curve for Python users accustomed to the more permissive,
 dynamic typing of standard libraries.
 Finally,
 while rds2py enables efficient data ingestion,
 it currently supports core classes;
 highly custom user-defined S4 objects may require additional configuration for mapping.
 BiocPy provides the necessary structure for stable and sustainable software development.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
BiocPy represents a maturation of the Python bioinformatics ecosystem.
 It acknowledges that while Python may be the language of the future for AI,
 Bioconductor remains the standard for statistical and biological data modelling.
 By building a bridge through shared data structures,
 BiocPy empowers developers to create tools that seamlessly traverse the R/Python divide,
 effectively enabling researchers to stop fighting their tools and focus on answering biological questions.
 It envisions a future where data flows freely between R and Python.
\end_layout

